role: Solo — design and build
period: 2021 — 2023
stack: [Java, JavaFX, PostgreSQL, Gson, JUnit 5]
repo: https://github.com/igor-siergiej/diet-planner
status: archived
---

## Problem

Calorie trackers count calories. They are much worse at the question that
actually matters for a diet: am I short of anything? Micronutrient targets are
not one number — they differ by sex, by age band, and by whether someone is
pregnant or breastfeeding — and most tools either ignore them or flatten them to
a single adult default.

## Approach

A desktop diary built on a real food-composition dataset. Log meals from the
dataset, and the app totals every nutrient for the day and compares it against
the reference intakes for that profile, including upper limits as well as
targets.

## Architecture

JavaFX with FXML screens and a controller per screen, all extending a shared
`BaseScreenController`.

The nutrition data is 2,887 foods, each carrying up to 53 nutrients, loaded from
JSON with Gson. Reference intakes are separate JSON files per cohort —
`men14to18`, `men19to50`, `men50plus`, the corresponding women's bands,
`pregnant`, `breastfeeding` — plus a `maximumDoses` file for upper limits.
`DailyIntake` picks the right target set from the `Profile` (sex, birth date,
activity level, pregnancy flags) rather than hard-coding one.

The domain is `Profile` → `Diary` → `Entry`, where an entry is a `Meal` or a
`Food`. Accounts and saved profiles live in PostgreSQL over JDBC; profiles are
serialised to a JSON string in the `users` row. Passwords are salted and hashed
before storage. A `GraphViewController` plots daily energy over time as a JavaFX
`LineChart`.

## Decisions and trade-offs

**Reference intakes are data, not code.** Each cohort is its own JSON file that
`DailyIntake` loads by name. Encoding the thresholds as `if (age > 50)` branches
would have been fewer files and would have made every revision to a guideline a
code change and a rebuild.

**A profile is stored as a serialised document inside a relational row.** The
`users` table holds `email`, `password`, `salt` and a `profiledata` string. This
is the wrong answer for querying — there is no way to ask "which profiles are
short on iron" without deserialising everything. It was the right answer for the
app that existed, which only ever loads one profile at a time on login, and it
kept the schema to a single table.

**Screen navigation is an explicit undo/redo stack.** `UndoRedoStack` holds the
screen history so back and forward are real operations rather than each
controller knowing which screen preceded it. It made adding screens cheap; it
also means the stack, not the controllers, owns a piece of state that is easy to
get out of step.

## Outcome

Finished as a university-era project and archived; the last substantive work was
in 2022. Eight JUnit 5 test classes cover the profile, diary, daily-intake,
validation, meal and password-hashing logic; the eight screen controllers all
extend one `BaseScreenController`.

What I would do differently is obvious in hindsight and is the direct ancestor of
how I build now: the JDBC calls are static methods called straight from
controllers, so there is no seam to test or swap. Every app since puts a
repository interface there.
